\subsection{Temporal Embedding Representation Analysis}
\label{sec:cosine_evolution}

To understand how the trained $\dt$-embedding creates vulnerability, we analyze the cosine similarity between $e(\dt{=}1)$ and $e(\dt{=}2)$ across training (exp\_065). Trained embeddings learn \emph{near-opposite} representations: $\cos(e_1, e_2) = {-}0.87$ to ${-}0.94$ at 25K steps across three configurations (Walker 008d\_s3, Cheetah 010b\_s0, Walker 059\_s5), persisting at ${-}0.70$ to ${-}0.86$ at 75K steps. In contrast, frozen embeddings maintain near-zero similarity ($-0.15$ to $+0.07$, constant throughout training). The near-antipodal learned representations explain the large reward prediction gap (\S\ref{sec:f2}): $\dt{=}2$ conditioning pushes the RSSM into a qualitatively different region of latent space, producing systematically biased predictions rather than minor perturbations.

