\caption{Temporal ordering of fidelity degradation and value overestimation (exp\_066, $n{=}1$). \textbf{Left axis}: single-step fidelity ratio (SS, blue) grows steadily from $1.05\times$ to $1.68\times$. \textbf{Right axis}: value-to-eval ratio (VE, red) remains near zero until 40K, then spikes. The ${\sim}$10--15K step lag supports the causal direction: fidelity gap $\to$ value overestimation. Replication with a second seed (exp\_066b) is in progress.}
\label{fig:temporal_ordering}
\end{figure}

The $\dt{=}1$ prediction MAE decreases monotonically ($0.090 \to 0.047$) as the world model improves, but $\dt{=}2$ MAE plateaus at ${\sim}0.08$. The growing fidelity gap is therefore not caused by $\dt{=}2$ predictions worsening, but by the model selectively improving at $\dt{=}1$ while failing to transfer that improvement to $\dt{=}2$---consistent with the trained $\dt$-embedding learning maximally differentiated representations (\S\ref{sec:cosine_evolution}) that prevent cross-scale generalization.

\textbf{Limitation.} These results are from a single seed ($n{=}1$). A second seed (exp\_066b) is in progress to assess replicability.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}
